    "question": {
      ":question/id": "93c73612-8a6c-5442-942a-82d98b2c6713",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=6$ and $Q(x)=x{e}^{-6x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{6x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y) & ={e}^{6x}\\cdot x{e}^{-6x} \\\\ {e}^{6x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{e}^{6x}\\cdot y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{6x}) & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{6x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{-6x}}{2}+C{e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
